 2.\emdash  The boy gave the bicycle to be repaired to the smith.\par 3.\emdash  The bicycle has been repaired by the smith; its wheel had been bent by the boy.\par 4.\emdash  The bicycles have been repaired by the smiths: their wheels had been bent by the boys.\par 5.\emdash  Do you see the boy? Do you[pl.] see the boys? \par 6.\emdash  The boys enter (}{\i\f211 into}{\f208 ) the bedrooms.\par 7.\emdash  The boys' clothes remain in the bedrooms.\par 8.\emdash  Give[pl.] cakes to the boys.\par \par }{\i\f211 In this exercise, try to distinguish the neuter plurals in \endash }{\b\i\f212 a}{\i\f211  from the nominative singulars of the 1st declension.\par }{\scaps\f208 Answer}{\f208 : }{\i\f211 Nom. pl.}{\f208 : }{\b\f210 cub}{\f208 i}{\b\f210 cula}{\f208 , }{\b\f210 cr}{\f208 u}{\b\f210 stula}{\f208 ; }{\i\f211 nom. sg.}{\f208 : }{\b\f210 b}{\f208 i}{\b\f210 rota}{\f208 , }{\b\f210 r}{\f208 o}{\b\f210 ta}{\f208 , }{\b\f210 dist}{\f208 o}{\b\f210 rta}{\f208 .\par \par }{\i\f211 Are you starting to get used to the Latin word order? Phrase }{\b\i\f212 2}{\i\f211  of the exercise is an example of normal word order}{\f208  \par 1}{\f208\super st}{\f208  }{\scaps\f208 Subject}{\f208  (}{\b\f210 p}{\f208 u}{\b\f210 er}{\f208 )\par 2}{\f208\super nd}{\f208  }{\scaps\f208 Indirect object}{\f208  (}{\b\f210 f}{\f208 a}{\b\f210 bro}{\f208 )\par 3}{\f208\super rd}{\f208  }{\scaps\f208 Direct object}{\f208  (}{\b\f210 b}{\f208 i}{\b\f210 rotam }{\f208 with its attribute }{\b\f210 refici}{\f208 e}{\b\f210 ndam}{\f208 )\par 4}{\f208\super th}{\f208  and in the end the }{\scaps\f208 verb}{\f208  (}{\b\f210 d}{\f208 e}{\b\f210 dit}{\f208 )\par }{\i\f211 \par }{\f208 Today let's content ourselves with improving our knowledge of the words of the 2}{\f208\super nd}{\f208  declension (gen. in -}{\b\i\f212 i}{\f208 ) Have you recognized:\par \emdash  masculines in -}{\b\f210 us}{\f208 : }{\b\f210 m}{\f208 a}{\b\f210 lleus}{\f208  (nom.), }{\b\f210 dist}{\f208 o}{\b\f210